---
// What filing makes of a few ordinary messages.
---

<figure class="diagram" aria-label="What filing keeps from three messages">
  <div class="diagram-box">
    <strong>[m1] Maya, in #kitchen, Tue 09:14</strong>
    <p>
      My sister Grace moved to Leeds last month, so we'll be up there a lot.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-row">
    <div class="diagram-box accent">
      <strong>Note · on Grace's page</strong>
      <p>Grace, Maya's sister, lives in Leeds.</p>
      <small>Quote: "My sister Grace moved to Leeds" · said by Maya in #kitchen</small>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <strong>Journal line · dated last month</strong>
      <p>Maya's sister Grace moved to Leeds.</p>
      <small>Points back at m1's exchange.</small>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-box">
    <strong>[m2] Maya, in a DM, Tue 09:30</strong>
    <p>Convert 250 dollars to euros for me.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-box quiet">
    <strong>Nothing</strong>
    <p>A request that was simply carried out says nothing lasting about Maya.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-box">
    <strong>[m3] Maya, in a DM, Tue 09:41</strong>
    <p>Every weekday at 8am, send me the top cycling news.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-box quiet">
    <strong>At most a journal line</strong>
    <p>
      The reply set up a schedule, so the schedule holds the job and how to do
      it. Memory never keeps a copy that could go stale.
    </p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Notes hold what stays true; journal lines make the conversation findable
    again; what the Familiar can look up itself isn't kept.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
